WEBVTT - development: tools built and what they verified

NOTE
One cue per item, one second long, in the order the items were settled; the time is the order, not a clock. The identifier names the item; the text says what it is, when, and where it lives. A cue that is replaced is not deleted: a later cue says what replaced it.

zbuf
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
Plan 9 C buffer framing: 117 unbound files byte-equal to ZIGUAROTH buffer.js.

zwsn
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.000
WSN parser by WSN's own grammar; every .wsn produced is read by it.

conv
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.000
proofs/js and unbound HTML JS MERMAID SH SVG to WSN: 447 files, each reads back line for line and parses.

book
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Coq proof book: 70 registry modules compile; omi_pi_proof.v (dev-docs) breaks at line 3980, CD2_mul_matches_quaternion incomplete; all 71 in WSN with CHECKED [ BREAKS UNCHECKED ].

proofs-port
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.000
I-series checks to Plan 9 C, catalog.json as the register: I-108 to I-127b ported, 0 failing; JavaScript-host assertions reported as host, not ported. NEXT: the rest, from the standalone files (the catalogue owns them).

busybox
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.000
12 busybox checks as rc with Plan 9 C tools (rev, svgswitch, fano, wilson, tetra): 12 of 12 pass.

manboy
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Knuth's man or boy in Plan 9 C: k = 0..10 gives 1 0 -2 0 1 0 1 -1 -10 -30 -67.

zlazy
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Lazy WSN evaluator: wsn/logic.wsn (XOR XNOR AND OR FULLADDER, ADD as two carry states) and wsn/modal.wsn answer as expected.

zrender
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.000
Renders a term per codex index: _CODICES XOR/XNOR against the zeroth, _CODEX[0] braille and Aegean, _CODEX[1] WSN.

euler
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.000
tcll2wsn: the public-domain EULER compiler grammar (Christopher section 4.1) to WSN, 38 productions.

tetra-ecc
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.000
ztetra: four files as a tetrahedron; faces close, any file from any other and their edge, P recovers any one. zsync and distrib.rc: each file lost and rebuilt across ramfs 9P servers.

handshake
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.000
zhand: set parity (same blocks), positional parity (where), shortest rot/rev/swap to sync; zperm: n indices, n! configurations, (n-1)! rotation cycles; rotation and rev reach 2n.

codependent
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.000
zcodep: every edge between files is the XOR of their two independent signatures against a zeroth (the zeroth cancels).

blob
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:14.000
zblob: Leningrad Genesis 1 as one 65,536-bit card (6,486 bytes), braille and stars-and-bars read back; zplaces: 6 of the 4-cube's 16 place subsets used.

tries
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:15.000
ztrie: the seven ZIGUAROTH/_* folders as WSN Patricia tries; 485 leaf paths, each a sentence; NOT/ON/DISK none.

frame
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.000
zframe: 9P server at /n/frame, 8 segments (the characters, seeded by consonants XOR YHWH, folded to 16 bits), tick mod 60; returns at 8, 60, both at 120. OPEN: how a frame interpolates over HTML, verified, breadboard, public narrative.

zkey
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.000
Six keys from the zeroth (reversed, XNORed), agreed order: only it brings all 8 files back; histogram over 719 others 0:212 1:207 2:139 3:93 4:41 5:19 6:7 7:1. Agreement, not protection.

zvtt
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:18.000
zvtt (vtt.rc): light-garden's 11 canon series written as WebVTT in VTT/NARRATIVE, 2,439 cues, each count equal to its canon's events, all passing the WebVTT check. narrative.py reads the canon on the host. Voices: the manifest's fano-points promoted to tags. Colours: the light letters of their fano-lines. Cue identifier: canon file and line, as timeline.ndjson's node_id. Times: the canon's t/timestamp; seq order where only seq is given. OPEN: region layout (2 columns by point) is zvtt's, not the canon's.

zvtt-tick
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.000
Replaces zvtt's timing: the canon's t/timestamp is UTC, which I-79 rules out (rosetta/src/trace.vtt.ts, I-84: one cue per tick). Each cue is now one tick in canon order. The tick is the PL360 rotation over the predeclared R0 R1 R2 and FLAG, starting at the Aegean 10100 10101 10102; reading forward sets FLAG, RESET(FLAG) after each step, and each cue is wrapped in the register state as classes (&lt;c.𐄁.𐄂.𐄀&gt;). Checked on all 11 series: each cue is one FLAG-set step from the one before, and reading back by the ELSE branch returns 10100 10101 10102. OPEN: what FLAG holds besides the reading direction; 28 (the time scale) is not yet in the step.

zvtt-slots
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.000
zvtt tracks each agent on its 16 files the way rosetta/src/agent.ts tracks the conversation: cue n lands on slot 1 + (n-1) mod 14, named as a class on the voice (Fano_point_5 is public/characters/SOLOMON/Fano_point_5.svg); 14 lit closes a round with a NOTE at place 15, Hasse_XNOR. 2,179 voiced cues, every slot equal to agent.ts slotOf; only SPEAKER completes rounds (154). OPEN: cue length, 1 tick (trace.vtt.ts, I-84) or 8 seconds (walkthrough.ts CUE_SECONDS, one per ruler slot).

zvtt-observation
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.000
Replaces zvtt's registers: R0 R1 R2 are the Aegean separators 10100 10101 10102 observing YHWH, YAHWEH, ADONAI. Over each cue's first 11 code points (its window, before a line break), ruby: in Aegean the window XOR R0's zeroth code point by code point, joined by R0's separator; in braille the window's bytes XOR the nearest of GOD IS WORD, WORD IS GOD, GOD OF WORD, WORD OF GOD (fewest bits flipped, first on a tie). Its IS or OF sets FLAG for the turn, then RESET(FLAG). Literal code points, no letter class (WSN leaves letter undefined). All 2,439 cues recomputed independently on the host, alike. OPEN: the window (first 11, or sliding); the step difference between cues cancels the zeroth, (A⊕Z)⊕(B⊕Z) = A⊕B.

zcue
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.000
zcue (cues.rc): 17 WSN files as WebVTT in VTT/SVG, SH, JS, MERMAID, one cue per LITERAL, one tick each, 3,686 cues, all valid. No position written: JS/cue-position.js places each cue when the track is read, at its literal's offset in the source buffer (searched from where the one before ended): exact, line = row (offset &gt;&gt; 8 &amp; 255) and position = column (offset &amp; 255) of the 65,536, as Media Spectrum DevTools' grid; or hypotenuse, position = hypot(row, column). Every offset equals the source line's start. OPEN: offsets past 65,535 (visual-proofs, Difference_DNA_RNA) wrap to page 1, and the page is not shown.

vtt-type
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.000
PLAN9/src/TYPES gains vtt as text/vtt, with no charset: the WebVTT registration has no parameters and a track is always UTF-8. All 34 tracks begin 57 45 42 56 54 54 20 (WEBVTT and a space), a registered magic number; cue-position.js gives 7,372 placements, none outside 0..100 (no IndexSizeError).

zvtt-window-timing
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.000
The observation window plays inside its tick: position k of the 11 starts at k*1000/11 ms after the cue's start, as a timestamp tag before the k-th code point of the base, the k-th Aegean value and the k-th braille cell, so ::cue(:past) and ::cue(:future) follow it. All 2,439 cues checked: every sub-step at k/11 of its tick, strictly inside it, and the observation unchanged. Meant to be read by a canvas handed to OffscreenCanvas or to breadboard as well as by a video element.

space-floor
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.000
space/src/plane.ts's floor ported to Plan 9 C (PLAN9/space/floor.c, src/space.rc) with draw(2) and event(2), on the guest's own screen: the twelve sectors of 30, the rim, the eight characters at their hues (characters.ts), each stepping gnomon.ts's K once a second from its seed; button 1 selects the nearest (plane.ts nearest). All eight states at tick 12 equal gnomon.ts's. Follows space's numbering (5 ENOCH, 6 SPEAKER, 7 GENESIS), which differs from the canon manifest's (5 Genesis, 6 Enoch, 7 Speaker): OPEN.

observer-mach
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:26.000
The Observer (PLAN9/space/observe.c, src/observe.rc) attaches to the running floor with mach(2): crackhdr and syminit on /proc/pid/text, lookup of ch and tick, attachproc on /proc/pid/mem. Reads each character's state (equal to gnomon.ts at that tick); interjects by stop, put2, start. Checked: SOLON set to 0001 steps to 4017 = K(0001) and returns to it 8 ticks later, the others undisturbed.

observer-constraints
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.000
observe.c takes constraints as MediaStreamTrack.applyConstraints does, without writing: exact, min, max mandatory over the 8 states of the character's K orbit (none meets them: OverconstrainedError naming the constraint), ideal the nearest by bits flipped; prints constraints, settings (the state now) and the chosen state with its ticks. Checked against gnomon.ts on SOLOMON's orbit: ideal 0000 in 6 ticks, F5F5 the only state over F000, none in 6000-6FFF, E2B3 and 0001 not in the orbit. An interjection (a write) can move a character to another orbit; a constraint never does.

zvtt-camera
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.000
VOICES holds each agent's hue from dome-leds.ndjson (an unsaturated light: no hue, the camera); zvtt numbers the agents from the camera (-p turn, -f flip), places each REGION by that number, and colours each voice hsl(hue, 70%, 55%). All tracks' NOTEs say a cue is one animation frame. 28 tracks regenerated in Plan 9; every cue unchanged, only the headers. The dome lists one stray Observer light, violet 300 saturated, beside its white one: left out.

zcast
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:29.000
The stack element (CSP, E empty and F full) ported to Wirth syntax notation (PLAN9/wsn/stack.wsn) with its letters left undefined, so it executes nothing until cast. zcast writes a zeroth's cast through terms.mjs's feature pipeline (consonants upper, vowels lowered 0x20, digits kept): YHWH gives CONSONANT "Y" "H" "W" and no VOWEL; YAHWEH adds VOWEL "a" "e". zlazy runs each sentence under both (cast.rc): 14 of 14 as expected. YHWH's skeleton runs under both casts, YAHWEH's only under its own, and a vowel taken in under YHWH matches nothing. OPEN: the figure's exchange with the neighbouring element (get, put) is garbled in the paste and not ported.

stack-server
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.000
The stack element live (PLAN9/src/stack.c, stack.rc): a lib9p server at /n/stack with cast, in, out and state, the live form of cast.rc's grammar. Before a cast nothing is taken (letter undefined). 15 of 15 as expected: identity (Y in, Y out; a in, a out, not A), fullness (a second write while full refused, a read while empty refused), the cast (YHWH refuses a; YAHWEH takes a and refuses A, since its cast holds the vowel lowered 0x20), and each zeroth's sequence through the element: YHWH's under both casts, YAHWEH's only under its own. The element changes no character; the 0x20 is the cast's.

omi-wsn
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.000
types: OMI.WSN, Wirth syntax notation typed over Byte with hidden constructors: parse (Wirth's seven productions), render (one production a line), accepts (as zlazy), cast (terms.mjs's pipeline). Tests give Plan 9's answers: zcast's casts exactly, cast.rc's stack sentences, WSN in itself read back unchanged, lazy.rc's logic and adder. List operations are folds: right where the search stops at the first whole match, left where the zeroth's order is kept.

accepts-memo
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.000
OMI.WSN.accepts takes a step only when the question reaches it and never twice: a table keeps each production's ends at each place; expanded lists them. Their number equals zlazy's for all 8 stack sentences under both casts: 16 of 16 (4 to 26).

zaudit
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.000
zaudit (audit.rc) writes light-garden audit's contract as WSN: canonical JSON, the audit's 94 top-level keys only, ERRORRECORD a record with a key naming an error. All 138 features and traces records are in the contract; ERRORRECORD holds for 0 of 75 goldens and 39 of 63 negatives. OMI.WSN normalizes the grammar unchanged and gives the same 276 answers; an unknown key, white space and a leading zero are refused.

audit-negatives
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.000
24 of 63 audit negatives carry no error key: 11 trace negatives state negation by value (expected_exists false, expected_broken true), and 13 feature negatives are auto-generated-coverage filler with expected true, the same shape as golden filler: they assert nothing negative.

guest-frame
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.000
Stage 1 of the XR view: PLAN9/frames.py serves the guest's own screen (a QEMU screendump as PNG, about 80 ms a frame) and the VTT tracks on 127.0.0.1:8790; boxedPINE space/guest.html draws the frames to a canvas, whose captureStream is a video carrying the narrative track, on a plane in three.js: any angle by drag or ?angle=, VR by VRButton. Seen in headless Chrome at 0 and 60 degrees with the floor ticking and the cues firing. Stage 2, the agents from any angle, needs the state served from Plan 9 over 9P.

agentd
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:36.000
Stage 2 of the XR view: space/agentd.c (agentd.rc, under aux/listen1 on tcp 8000, forwarded to the host's 127.0.0.1:18000) reads the running floor with mach(2): GET /agents (read, the default) as JSON, GET /constrain (asks, never writes), POST /interject (the only write; a GET there is 405). Checked: at tick 24 every agent at its seed; constrain gave SOLOMON ideal E2B3 in 6 ticks; an interjection of E2B3, from SOLOMON's own orbit, read back as 2849 = K(E2B3) at the next tick. boxedPINE space/guest.html shows the agents on the floor at their hues with live states; the guest frame and agentd agree.

step-shared
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:37.000
The PL360 turn of R0 R1 R2 (YHWH YAHWEH ADONAI) by FLAG moved out of zvtt.c into src/step.c and step.h, unchanged; zvtt.c and stepd.c share it. The 11 narrative tracks regenerated through it are byte-identical to the committed ones (0 differ).

stepd
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.000
src/stepd.c: the shared step() as a 9P file server at /n/step: R0 R1 R2 read their zeroth and separator; writing IS to FLAG is one turn forward, OF one back, then RESET(FLAG); anything else is refused and nothing turns. step.rc, live: 14 of 14, among them three forward and three back return to the start, and stepd equals steptest (the same step()) for three scripted sequences. No port forward: the host has no 9P client yet.

omi-dom-merge
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.000
github omi-dom: Grok's two lines joined. The building version (22-23 September, from /home/main/Programs/omi-dom-stack) kept whole on branch snapshot-2026-09-22; main (the package version) gains only what it lacked: 11 modules, 12 docs, client/circuit.html. npm test now awaits every selfTest in its own process: 46 pass, 0 fail, 2 without selfTest.

prec-c
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.000
src/prec.c is prec.awk in Plan 9 C, line for line (wsn.awk's file form only). Over the same 447 files: the same 35,738 Wirth-Weber relations, each held in the same set of files; only the order of each file list differs, awk's being its hash order (precheck.rc).

zprec
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.000
src/zprec.c decides the four conditions of a simple precedence grammar on any WSN: no erasing rules ([ ] and { } derive the empty string in BNF), no useless rules, one relation per pair, unique right sides. wsn/step.wsn (STEP = SET FLAG TURN RESET) and wsn/turns.wsn (what stepd accepts, TURNS = TURN | TURN TURNS, without the empty string) pass all four. stack.wsn fails 1 (EMPTY) and 2 (the HELD productions are undefined until cast); the audit contract fails 1 (8 productions use [ ] or { }) and 2 (ERRORRECORD's rules unreachable from RECORD). WSN hides the empty string; it is still in the language.

omi-dom-pss
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.000
First omi-dom module ported: shared/projective-swap-space.js as src/zpss.c (pss.rc). The module is a table of labels plus one computation, the 3! orderings of the layout unit (BL BO BPE). zpss gives its six orderings in its order, its role table exactly (both checked against the JavaScript's own output, in/pss) and its ten selfTest assertions; several of those assert the module's own constants. Against stepd, every sequence of up to three turns reaches 3 of the 6 orderings, the rotations (BL BO BPE, BO BPE BL, BPE BL BO); the three single swaps are never reached. The module does not reduce to the turn: the turn is its even half, the swaps (swap16-like) are the other.

omi-dom-fano
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.000
Second omi-dom module ported: shared/fano-7d-gate.js as src/zfano.c (fano.rc). Every byte gives what the module gives (256 of 256: phase, parity, the closure once and twice, the chirality locks, the strict gate, the line), the Fano axioms hold over its lines (each pair of the 7 points on one line, each point on 3), and its sixteen assertions pass, its two token forms under Plan 9 regexp(2). Finding: its 13-mask involution cannot fail (any XOR cascade applied twice cancels), and the 13 masks XOR to 0x00, so one pass is already the identity for all 256 bytes. Neither the turn nor an exchange: its own primitive, an XOR fold to zero over a Fano incidence table.

omi-dom-pe
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:44.000
Third omi-dom module ported, the first with host dependencies: shared/parallel-engine.js as src/zpe.c (pe.rc). SharedArrayBuffer is memory shared between libthread procs, Atomics is cas32, postMessage a channel. Its twelve assertions pass and its values equal the JavaScript engine's (CAS hit old 1 epoch 1, miss old 99 epoch 2, bindApplyEval old 2 epoch 3, digest 37 epoch 4, ruler 99 77 3 4 5 6 7 8). Added a race its selfTest lacks: 100 rounds of 8 procs claiming one cell, exactly one winner each time. Its column: compare-exchange, the observer or agent operation, not the turn.

proof-cas-race
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:45.000
proofs/proof.c compareexchange read the cell, then cas32; if another proc changed it in between, cas32 failed and it returned exp, as though the exchange had happened. Harmless single-threaded, wrong under concurrency: it now reads again. The 20 I-records still pass, 0 failing assertions.

worker-design-trap
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.000
For worker-based modules (parallel-engine done; system-probe and worker-barrier still to port): every worker receives every run, and a worker that takes the lock after another finished sees IDLE and still answers. A port must match each answer on the command posted, as the JavaScript does, or a late IDLE answer replaces the real one.

omi-dom-pcas
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.000
Fourth omi-dom module ported: shared/polytope-cas.js as src/zpcas.c (pcas.rc). Every byte gives the module's table (256 of 256) and its fifteen assertions pass. Finding: its compareExchange compares nothing; each step replaces eval with apply(vertex). apply is the XOR of three bit permutations, on bit positions i to i^1, i^3, i^7: boxedPINE's swap16, swap32, swap64, there on byte positions, here on bits, summed rather than composed. Over GF(2) the sum squares to the identity, so apply without a carry is a real involution (256 of 256), linear (65536 of 65536 pairs), and two passes stay the identity for exactly the 16 carries it leaves fixed. Its selfTest checks none of this. Column: the swap algebra, an involution; not compare-exchange.

omi-dom-obs
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Fifth omi-dom module ported: shared/observer-sync.js as src/zobs.c (obs.rc); its four assertions pass. Finding: its centroid invariance cannot fail; invariants() computes the digest once and copies it, consistent true, into every observer's entry, never reading per observer and never using a position, so all_consistent holds whenever one observer is registered. The port adds the check it names: one writer changes the ruler 2000 times, marking each change through an epoch by cas32 (odd while writing); four observers fold their own digest and read again when the epoch was odd or moved. About 229 million reads, 0 disagreed with the writer; the falsifier, the same observers without the second epoch read, disagreed 25 times in about 224 million, so the check has teeth. Column: consistent reading under a writer, distinct from parallel-engine's exclusion between writers.

omi-dom-hzc
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:49.000
Sixth omi-dom module, as its contract: hit-zones-cues writes WebVTT, so its contract is the timestamps and timing lines it writes, wsn/vtttime.wsn (hours of two or more digits, minutes and seconds 00 to 59, milliseconds exactly three digits). Of 423 timestamps from its own toVttTime, 278 are in the contract and 145 are not: every refusal has four millisecond digits (toVttTime(59.9996) is 00:00:59.1000), because it rounds the milliseconds apart from the seconds; its selfTest never meets a fraction that rounds up. zlazy and OMI.WSN agree on all 425 answers (hs/Contract.hs), as does an independent regular expression. zprec: not a simple precedence grammar (HOURS gives DIGIT DIGIT two relations; SIX and DIGIT share right sides).

omi-dom-grex
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Seventh omi-dom module: regex-constraints, the constraint set G of 23 regular expressions, no selfTest; its goldens are its own admits() over 275 tokens. Its 17 without backreferences are wsn/g.wsn; its 6 with backreferences are src/zgrex.c, since DEFLECT, REFLECT, INFLECT and MNEMONIC repeat an unbounded group (the copy language, not context-free: no WSN can hold them) and Plan 9's regexp(2) has no backreferences (AXIS and PALINDROME are finite, but tens of thousands of alternatives). Both answer as the module for every token (grex.rc), and OMI.WSN (hs/Bits.hs) gives zlazy's bits exactly.

omi-dom-poly
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:51.000
Eighth and ninth omi-dom modules: polytope-9d-inject and polytope-10d-infer as src/zpoly.c (poly.rc). Every vertex gives the modules' tables (apply9 1024 of 1024, apply10 2048 of 2048) and cascade8910 over a 16x16x16 grid gives all 4096 rows with its 16 locks; the 27 assertions pass, 5 of them type checks or true-or-false that cannot fail. Finding: widening changes the algebra. At 10 bits swap64 is i to 9-i, not a translation, so swap32 and swap64 no longer commute: apply9 is linear and invertible but no involution, order 6, and two applications return only 000 155 2AA 3FF. At 11 bits swap16_10 has no partner for bit 10 and drops it: apply10 maps 2048 vertices onto 1024 and loses information; two applications return 000 020 050 070. The selfTests never give a vertex with bit 10 set, and isometricLock reads only the low byte. Column: a second algebra (order 6), and at 11 bits not a permutation.

omi-dom-probe
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.000
Tenth omi-dom module: system-probe as src/zprobe.c (probe.rc). Its four assertions pass and none can fail: every probe writes ok: true as a constant (busybox ok with no busybox, the codex ok when absent), so healthy is true on every system. The port probes Plan 9 itself and is ok only when it read what it names: osversion 2000, sysname helix, cputype 386, /dev/swap, descriptors 0 to 2. The falsifier, the same probes under a root holding nothing, gives healthy false. The codex probe keeps the module's rule (absent is still ok) and finds no /lib/codex in the guest.

omi-dom-bar
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.000
Eleventh omi-dom module: worker-barrier as src/zbar.c (bar.rc), procs posting (index, result) on a channel, the parent XORing at the nth post. Its two assertions pass with the module's values (partials 0 7 14 21, digest 28), and they are real ones. The race: 100 rounds of 8 procs finishing in random order, each result a different bit, give the full digest every round. The falsifier, finishing at the first post instead of the nth, disagrees in 100 of 100. Column: a barrier; partials kept by index, so the order of finishing cannot reach the digest.

omi-dom-pipe
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:54.000
Twelfth omi-dom module: constraint-pipeline as src/zpipe.c (pipe.rc), no selfTest. All 23 inputs (text, binary, a BOM, a surrogate, an overlong, a character past U+FFFF) give the module's five layer counts. Findings: admissible is true for any input (it checks matchCount >= 0); the dual mode is two different readings of the same bytes, 13 of 15 text inputs giving other counts when forced through the binary path (text counts characters over every line, in UTF-16 units; binary counts runs in the first line only, splitting on LF or CR only when no CR LF is present). The records that already hold the ground: I-144b (the /A-Z0-9/ block), I-151 (RegExp through Proxy/Reflect), I-186 (ASCII indices pass through UTF-8 as themselves). Contract: wsn/pipe.wsn, the delimiter layer as the regex states it, and wsn/pipeline.wsn, as the text path applies it, unambiguous. A WSN literal is one line (zlazy refuses an LF inside quotes), so CR and LF stay identifiers without productions, as Wirth leaves character undefined; zlazy answers on the module's 26 text lines as a printable-ASCII expression does, and zprec fails both on conditions 1 and 2 (erasing rules, CR and LF unproductive) before ambiguity is reached.

yhwh-six-pointings
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.000
2026-10-28, a look, not a record: the six Leningrad Codex presentations of the Name listed in Wikipedia's Tetragrammaton article (pasted by the author; second-hand, not yet read from the codex), run through ZIGUAROTH _ARCHIVE/Expressions/mask.js as six witnesses: node _ARCHIVE/Expressions/mask.js GEN2.4=יְהוָה GEN3.14=יְהֹוָה JUDG16.28=יֱהֹוִה GEN15.2=יֱהוִה 1KGS2.26=יְהֹוִה EZEK24.24=יְהוִה. All 15 pairs have letter mask 0000. The marks differ in three cells only: the yod (shewa or hataf segol, bit 8), the first he (holam or none, bit 4), the vav (qamats or hiriq, bit 2); the final he never. The six are six corners of a 3-bit cube, Gen 2:4 000, Ezek 24:24 001, Gen 3:14 010, 1 Kgs 2:26 011, Gen 15:2 101, Judg 16:28 111, and each pair's mark mask is the XOR of its corners (Judg 16:28 with 1 Kgs 2:26: 100, mask 8). Absent: 100 and 110, hataf segol with qamats. I-229, in/figures/YHWH.txt and YHWHELOHIM.txt and _CATALOG/YHWH/yhwh.wsn already hold the method and the Deut 29:1 and Gen 2:4 witnesses; these six become figures only when read from the Westminster Leningrad Codex at their verses (the Name only, never the verse text), which waits on the author.

