check
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.000
metaverse-build's checkpoints ported native, after zones (the author: "lets proceed, Well Done"; asked how, the author chose "Literal only"): PLAN9/src/zcheck.c, one subcommand for each of runtime/checkpoint's tools, checkpoint (a zone's snapshot and the trace it was folded from), restore (the snapshot and the trace after it folded again), rolling (a checkpoint named by its id in a directory), prune (all but the last k), window (a trace's lines start to end folded from a base). The tools name a snapshot by its sha256 and date a checkpoint by the clock, against the rules here (no hash is identity, no wall clock), so the format changes: a checkpoint holds the snapshot literal itself, with zone, trace, snapshot path and last_index (its trace's non-blank lines less one), and no hash and no time; restore compares the snapshot's bytes with the literal, any difference HALT: corruption, exit 3, and a checkpoint missing a field is refused; prune orders by last_index, then name, not by the clock. The fold is zworld, run as its own program (rfork and exec, its output the file), as the tools run apply-event.py. in/check written on the host by in/check/gen.py with the tools themselves, each case a list of steps (checkpoint, restore, rolling, prune, window, and tamper, a snapshot rewritten after its checkpoint): 72 cases, 52 written, 15 halted (corruption among them), 5 refused. They are checked where the formats agree: status, the stderr line, every snapshot byte for byte, each checkpoint's zone, trace, snapshot, last_index and id, and which files prune removes; the literal in each checkpoint is checked against its snapshot (zcheck verify). On the host with the libc shim and in the guest (src/check.rc), all 72 agree. The falsifier, the driver's own since the cases cannot tell trace position from name order: c9 checkpointed from a short trace and c10 from a long one, prune keeping 1: c10 stays and c9 goes, where name order keeps c9. The limit as zworld's: raw literals and digit strings. The spine's 30th phase: 30 of 30 pass, 30 of 30 the same twice. Next of the runtime: time, shards, sync transport, sync world, lattice.

time
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:42.000
metaverse-build's time engine ported native, literal as checkpoint (the author: "yes lets proceed"): PLAN9/src/ztime.c, one subcommand for each of runtime/time's tools. branch (branch.py: a new timeline of the parent's nodes, its root the parent): branch.py names it by a random uuid, branch-xxxxxxxx; here it is named by its address, TIMELINE/PARENT, so a branch of a branch is main/ck-2/ck-1 and two branches from one point are one literal; nothing in metaverse-build reads the id's shape (loadtest.sh only counts branches). materialize (materialize.py: a window of a trace replayed from a checkpoint's snapshot, by window-replay.py): from the checkpoint's literal, so its snapshot must be that literal's bytes, else HALT: corruption, as restore; the window is zcheck's, run as its own program, as materialize.py runs window-replay.py; it prints the snapshot where the tool prints its sha256. merge (merge-check.py: MERGE_OK, or HALT: MergeDenied): the worlds compared, then each checkpoint's literal against its snapshot's bytes and the two literals, where the tool compares sha256s, so its "hash mismatch" is "literal mismatch". in/time written on the host by in/time/gen.py with the tools themselves, their checkpoints by checkpoint.py and the port's by zcheck from the same steps: 148 cases (branch 40, materialize 51, merge 57), 87 written, 35 halted, 26 refused; the tool's ids checked for their shape, its branch files kept with the id the address. On the host with the libc shim and in the guest (src/time.rc), all 148 agree, every file byte for byte. The falsifier, ztime -n, which does not check a literal against its snapshot: of the 26 merges of a snapshot rewritten after its checkpoint, 17 go through, the other 9 being two traces of two results anyway. And materialize from a rewritten snapshot: HALT: corruption, nothing written, where materialize.py replays the rewritten file. Found in the tools: merge-check.py never reads the timelines' nodes, so the checkpoints merged need not be in either timeline, and MERGE.md's canonical, speculative and local are not checked; materialize replays its window over the checkpoint's result, not its base, so time-tests.sh applies its first event twice, which holds only because a repeated ENTITY_CREATE by the same owner changes nothing. The limit as zworld's: raw literals and digit strings. On the way, my fault: console.py types what it is given and sends no newline of its own; two commands typed without one (the world server's restart after check, recorded there as done, and the first time.rc run) sat on the console line unrun; the world server answering /tiles was the one already running. Cleared (^U does not erase there; a word appended made the line a syntax error, nothing run) and run again with the newline. The spine's 31st phase.

clock
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:43.000
The algorithmic clock ported native, I-248 (time-as-logic in global.vtt; the author: "yes both would work"): PLAN9/src/zclock.c. replay seed steps: kernel.py's replay, a line a tick (t, state, position, orbit, offset, phase, diff, and the band: width, density, texture), a tick the delta law with B[t mod 8] XORed in; sweep: how many of the 65,536 states are back after 8 ticks and after 16; spins n: for each prime q below n but 2 and 5, T = ord_q(10) and how many of the steps 1..T CyclicClock.v's recover_step gives back from encode_step, R = (10^T - 1)/q in libmp; block q: R's T digits. in/clock written on the host by in/clock/gen.py: replay and sweep by atomic-kernel's kernel.py itself, imported; spins and blocks by CyclicClock.v's definitions transcribed (is_prime, mult_order_10, cyclic_number, encode_step, modinv by trying, recover_step), there being no extraction of them. Sixteen seeds (0001 0000 FFFF 4242 AA55 55AA 1D1D 8000, and 0x1D1D's eight fixed points), 40 ticks each. On the host (the shim given a libmp over OpenSSL's BIGNUM) and in the guest (src/clock.rc): all 16 replays as kernel.py gives them; of 65,536 states none back after 8 ticks and all after 16; 166 primes below 1000, every step recovered but at 3 and 487, none there; the ten cyclic blocks of the whitepaper's table. The falsifier, zclock -b, no B injected: all 16 replays differ, and every state is back after 8. The spine's 32nd phase: 32 of 32 pass, 32 of 32 the same twice. Next of metaverse-build's runtime: shards, sync transport, sync world, lattice.

shard
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:44.000
metaverse-build's shards ported native, literal as checkpoint and time: PLAN9/src/zshard.c. bundle (bundle.py: OUTDIR/ZONE/ with the snapshot, trace and checkpoint copied byte for byte and manifest.json; prints the manifest's path; missing input: PATH, nothing written); restore (restore.py: manifest.json not found, missing bundle file: PATH, then each file checked in the order snapshot, trace, checkpoint: OK, or HALT: ... mismatch, exit 3). bundle.py's manifest keeps each file's sha256 and restore.py compares sha256s; here the manifest keeps each file's bytes as a JSON string (" \ and control bytes escaped, read back by Python's json to the same bytes) and restore compares bytes, so "hash mismatch" is "literal mismatch". The cost, stated: the manifest is as large as what it carries; a hash is short because it gives the bytes up, and the literal keeps them. in/shard written on the host by in/shard/gen.py with the tools themselves, each case a list of steps (bundle, restore, move a bundle to another node as shard-tests.sh copies it, tamper or drop a file in transit): 114 cases, 66 OK, 30 halted, 18 refused; checked: status, the stderr line, stdout, 198 bundled files byte for byte, 66 manifests' zone and file names, nothing written on a missing input. On the host with the libc shim (snprint added) all 114 at the first run; in the guest (src/shard.rc), all 114 agree. The falsifier, zshard -n, which does not compare the literals: all 30 bundles rewritten in transit restore as OK. On the way: in rc a subscripted variable takes no free caret after it, so $t/$a(3)/$f is two words; the first guest run's moves copied nothing and 48 cases failed (the falsifier then counted 0 of 30, its cases unreached); written $a(3)^/$f. Found in the tools: restore.py with a manifest missing a file's name joins the bundle directory with "", which exists, and then fails reading a directory (no case here). The limit: a file holding a NUL byte is outside the port's claim. The spine's 33rd phase: 33 of 33 pass, 33 of 33 the same twice. Next of metaverse-build's runtime: sync transport, sync world, lattice.

transport
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.000
metaverse-build's sync transport ported native (the author: "yes lets proceed"): PLAN9/src/zsyncw.c receive, after runtime/sync-transport/receive.sh: each line of standard input appended to the log but the empty ones. The transport's tools are cat into a fifo and nc (send.sh, receive.sh); its tests rest on sync-world's merge and apply, so the two components share one golden, in/sync, and have a driver each. in/sync written on the host by in/sync/gen.py with the tools themselves, against a copy of metaverse-build in the scratchpad (apply-merged.sh writes its temporaries into its own state directory): the world golden's events as envelopes {"peer","seq","event"} of one to three peers, put through chaos as chaos.sh does (dropped, duplicated, shuffled by a seeded random), sometimes a blank line or a last line without its newline, sent by send.sh into a real fifo that receive.sh reads, one receiver or two. In the guest (src/transport.rc) each send goes through a Plan 9 pipe into zsyncw receive: all 79 sends logged as receive.sh logged them. Found in the tool, and kept by the port, the contract calling the transport lossy: receive.sh reads with read -r, which does not read a last line without its newline, so that line is lost (ten cases). The spine's 34th phase.

sync
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.000
metaverse-build's sync world ported native: zsyncw merge (merge.sh: every envelope of a directory's .jsonl logs, ordered by peer, then seq, written compact with its keys in their order; its first 200 lines on standard output) and zsyncw apply (apply-merged.sh: the base copied, each envelope's event folded by zworld as its own program, a HALT logged "HALT i why" and the state kept, any other refusal ending it with the fold's exit; the snapshot printed where the tool prints its sha256). On the host (the shim's fprint corrected: it wrote every descriptor but 2 to standard output) and in the guest (src/sync.rc), all 61 cases agree: the merged log and merge's stdout, apply's exit, stderr line and stdout, both peers' snapshot and halts log byte for byte; 47 applied, 14 ended by a malformed event, 44 with halts, two peers one result. Found in the tools, each shown on the host first: merge.sh breaks a tie of peer and seq by os.listdir, the order the filesystem chooses, though SCHEMA.md names the order a deterministic total order; the port takes the logs' names, then their lines, and the cases hold no tie of two different envelopes; in the guest two logs with one peer and seq merge in name order whichever is written first, and the falsifier, zsyncw -l, taking the directory's order, gives two merged logs in a ramfs, which lists files as they were made (on the disk the guest's /tmp is bound to, the directory came back in name order and the falsifier did not fire, so it runs in a ramfs). And apply-merged.sh pastes each envelope into Python source between ''', where a JSON escape is read as Python's: an id q\"x stops it with a JSONDecodeError; zsyncw reads the envelope as JSON and folds it. My faults on the way: I named the port zsync.c, which was already PLAN9's XOR parity tool for distrib.rc, and wrote over it; tar's "can't make (sym)link" (the name listed twice in pack.sh) showed it; restored from git, the port named zsyncw.c; and the generator's last line without its newline fell on cases sent to two receivers, so none was made; corrected. The limit as zworld's: raw literals and digit strings. The spine's 35th phase: 35 of 35 pass, 35 of 35 the same twice. Next and last of metaverse-build's runtime: lattice.

lattice
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.000
metaverse-build's lattice ported native, the last of its runtime (the author: "lets proceed"): PLAN9/src/zlattice.c. observe (compiler/peer-observer.sh: each beacon line read appended to observed.jsonl and traced beacon_seen) and tick (reconcile/tick.sh: compiler/graph-basis-compiler.sh, seeds and observations into peergraph.json and basis.json, the lowest rtt first; compiler/plan-projector.sh, the basis into connection-plan.json and device-plan.json; and on a plan other than the last, a rebind traced and reconcile/rebind.sh run). Literal, as checkpoint: the tools' trace lines carry the clock ("t":`date +%s`) and the port's none; tick keeps the last plan itself where the tool keeps its sha256; the host's address is an argument where the projector asks the machine's routes (ip route get); rebind.sh, which starts the posix bus's TCP listener, is traced and not run. As the tools' sed, awk, sort and read do it: each field sed's last match on its line, so a nested "id" inside a beacon names a peer (inner-2 in the cases); seed files joined as cat joins them; a peer's fields the last non-empty; the nodes in sort -u's order; a node line split as read splits on a tab IFS, empty fields collapsing, a later field moving left; the basis by sort -nr, a tie by the whole line reversed; a line read only with its newline. in/lattice written on the host by in/lattice/gen.py with the tools themselves, each case in a root of its own, under LC_ALL=C with a stub ip and a stub rebind.sh: 48 cases, 80 ticks, 67 compiled and projected and 13 failed; checked 274 files byte for byte, the clock taken out, nothing written that the tools did not write. On the host and in the guest (src/lattice.rc), all 48 agree. The falsifier, zlattice -n, the plan not compared with the last: a rebind on an unchanged plan, and those 15 cases' routing.log differ. Found in the tools, each shown on the host: with no seed file, cat of the unmatched glob fails under pipefail and the compiler ends, its 2>/dev/null hiding only the message (the 13 failed ticks); the projector ends the same way when ip finds no route, so a machine without one cannot project; sort -u and the *.json glob follow the locale (under en_US: host10 host-1 Host-a host-b host_c; under C: Host-a host-1 host-b host10 host_c), so two hosts with two locales write two graphs from one set of beacons; an rtt with a leading zero is read by bash as octal (010 scores 99992, 08 ends the compiler), outside the port's claim. My fault, first: the first golden's stub ip exited 1, so every tick in it failed at the projector, and it held the observer's output and failed ticks only. On the author's VPS, OpenCode built a zlattice.c and lattice.rc against it, and DeepSeek guided that work through days of rc byte-matching; their port encoded the stub as the tool's behaviour (every tick exiting 1, no projector, no plan); its 13 stubborn failures came from the tab collapse and the sed matches against a golden that held half the pipeline; and DeepSeek's reading at the end, that "the lattice tools depend on a live, connected network", was the stub, not the tools. Their two files are kept in the scratchpad and in the author's archive (boxed _archive/_chat_history/"this what we have been working on"); the golden was made again with a stub ip that answers nothing (HOST_IP then 127.0.0.1), and the port written fresh against it. The spine's 36th phase: 36 of 36 pass, 36 of 36 the same twice. metaverse-build's runtime is ported: world, zones, checkpoint, time, shards, sync transport, sync world, lattice.

walk
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.000
The acceptance test walked, the last of the twin's steps (questions.vtt; the author's form in global.vtt digital-twin and replay-video; DeepSeek's relay of it, sent by the author: "Yes. Build twin.html?mode=walk in that form. Restart the guest floor and agentd first"): boxedPINE space/walk.html (boxedPINE b1a7740). Inside the frame: first person in the 3D Commons, W S A D to walk, Q E to turn, each step given to two workers on transferred canvases (twin3d-worker.ts, now taking a pose), Solomon a sphere in A and the glTF model in B, both live videos recorded and replayable with the narrative track. Outside the frame: the floor from above (plane.ts drawFloor), the walker and the path on it. Each step a literal line, the step, the keys held, the Plan 9 floor's tick and the eight states, offered as a file, so the walk replays without the keyboard or the floor. On ending, two new workers render the trace alone: A again must be A's frames byte for byte (replayed); B may differ from A only inside Solomon's region, the union of both icons' bounds rendered alone (the same, XNOR), and must differ wherever his icon shows, the scene drawn black around him with the depth test kept (separate, XOR). In headless Chrome, ?auto (a fixed path: north toward Metatron, round to Solomon, past him, back), the states from the Plan 9 floor through agentd, ticks 853 to 878: 71 steps, 71 of 71 frames of A replayed byte for byte, his icon showing in 15 steps and differing in all 15, 0 pixels differing outside his region, live and replayed: the walk holds; the same with the states stepped in the browser. The falsifier, ?falsify, a cube held before the eyes in B: 352,728 pixels outside his region, the walk does not hold. On the way: a first count took "in view" as his region on the screen, which at the edge of the view can be there with neither icon; 21 in view, 15 differing; the count is now of his icon itself. And the guest restarted after the host's reboot: the floor (space.rc -o, in graphics), agentd, the world server; agentd answered tick 0 while the floor's screen said 46: the event library's slaves are named floor too, each with its memory as it was when forked, and agentd took the first floor /proc listed; PLAN9/space/agentd.c now takes the lowest pid, the floor itself, which starts first (ticks 94, 96, 98 after). Left: the author's own walk, as a digital avatar.

walkthrough
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.000
The walk-through of the podcast (the author, 2026-11-01, of the four NotebookLM explainers in boxed _archive/_inbox/Podcast: "I think if we can walk through the podcast with a picture in picture of the offscreen canvas, shadow dom, offline audio context and the video elements and vtt cues as walk through of the picture in picture view of the 2d, 2.5d,3d and 4d space geometry of the PLan9 agent commons then i can verify the walkthrough from the walktrough of the video walkthrough"; asked what the 4D is, the author chose the steps as the fourth axis): boxedPINE space/walkthrough.html (boxedPINE 76eda3b). The podcast plays, chosen from disk with its .vtt (or ?media= and ?vtt=); its own cues are the walk's steps, each cue entered one step and once, the podcast's timeline the only clock; each step as in the walk (the shared parts now space/src/walker.ts): the keys held or ?auto's path, the Plan 9 floor's tick and eight states, Solomon a sphere in A and the glTF model in B, each an OffscreenCanvas in a worker. The picture in picture, a custom element with a shadow root: the four views on one canvas, 2D the floor from above with the walker's path, 2.5D the map tilted with each agent lifted by its state's low byte, 3D A from the walker's eyes, 4D every step's floor stacked by its step as a cone of cross-sections with each agent's and the walker's world line through them; its stream a video the browser can float over the podcast, and recorded, replayable with the podcast's cues. The eight voices rendered by an OfflineAudioContext from the trace, each cue's span its own. At the end all is rebuilt from the trace: A's frames byte for byte, B differing from A wherever his icon shows and nowhere outside his region, the last composite of the four views pixel for pixel, the voices sample for sample. In headless Chrome, Digital_Causality at four times its speed, the fixed path, the states from the Plan 9 floor (ticks 1667 to 1747): its 89 cues walked, 89 of 89 frames replayed, the four views the same, the eight voices (2,538,080 samples each) the same, his icon showing in 16 steps and differing in all 16, 0 pixels outside his region: the walk-through holds; ?falsify, a cube held before the eyes in B: 197,402 pixels outside his region, it does not hold. Found on the way: Chrome sums a node's inputs in no fixed order, so eight voices mixed by the offline renderer differ from render to render by a float's last place (18,054 of 32,000 samples, at most 6e-8, where one voice alone is exact); each voice now has its own channel, nothing summed in the renderer, the mix to listen to made in the page in the voices' order. And a cue entered twice at the start made 90 steps of 89 cues; each cue is now walked once. Not adopted from DeepSeek's draft: advancing a step on cuechange, which fires once for several cues at speed; each cue's own enter is used. The four episodes were checked against the record in conversation: the lattice tools "hallucinating connections" (DeepSeek's retracted reading; the stub, development.vtt lattice), "65,536-bit ... exactly 6,486 bytes" (8,192), the spine "exactly 25" (36 now), and 248 dimensions where "time permanently stops" (the record: width 248 never returns and cannot be undone, rank 243 of 248, but it steps forward) are not as the record has them.

portal
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Every page a portal to the same architecture (the author: "lets update documentation an vtt cues so that every html page can share text tracks and media streams between them so each page is a portal to the same underlying architecture"): boxedPINE space/src/portal.ts, included by all fifteen pages of space/ (boxedPINE 80659c4), documented in space/README.md (the portal) and AGENTS.md. One BroadcastChannel joins the open pages. The state: the pages elect one reader of the Plan 9 floor with a Web Lock; it asks agentd once a second and tells every page the tick and the eight states; when it closes another takes the lock; the hub, the twin and the walker (walk, walk-through) now read the floor through it, guest.html still reading agentd itself for its constrain and interject. The text track: the narrative WebVTT, one URL for every page; attachTrack tells every page each cue it enters (the twin's, the walk's and the walk-through's tracks go through it). The media streams: offer by name, watch from any page, the signalling over the channel and the stream over a local RTCPeerConnection, a MediaStream not crossing a channel; the twin offers its two views, the walk its two, the walk-through its four-view picture in picture. On each page the portal is a custom element in a shadow root: the shared tick and states, the cue and the page it came from, every room, the streams on offer. Checked in one headless Chrome with two pages, map and gate: one reader, both at tick 3269; a red canvas offered on map watched on gate, 219 20 30 where 220 20 30 was drawn (the video's encoding); a podcast cue entered on gate heard on map; the reader closed, the other took the lock (tick 3281); and walk.html?auto still holds (71 of 71), index.html reading the floor through the portal (ticks 3471, 3473). On the way: a hidden tab's media does not load (Chrome defers it), so the cue was played in the front page. Not adopted from DeepSeek's draft: a portal.vtt of invented cues ("portal.tick 1 · view: commons"; the shared track is the narrative's, the one clock of replay-video), a fixed master page polling on its own interval (the pages elect their reader), and a MediaStream sent through a BroadcastChannel (it cannot be; the channel carries the signalling). Also my leftovers, put right: I-248's js-docs cross-links had not been committed (proofs a64628b), and the testbed's I-series count had stayed at I-231's 104 since I-232 (now 121, all 126 tests passing). The bridge test fails while this session's agent bridge holds 127.0.0.1:8743, the port it binds.

synset-router
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:51.000
The 144 colour synsets as a static router, from DeepSeek's relay (sent by the author) and the author's own words in I-199, "the WordNet was a synset-based router". Read from public/polytron/colors.json: 144 synsets, all nouns, one root (entity), every parent among them, 143 parent edges: one tree, the hypernym the one relation. So between any two there is exactly one route, up to their lowest common ancestor and down: boxedPINE space/src/commons-agents.ts synsetRoute, read off the parents, no table (boxedPINE 4455654). Checked in src/testbed/space.test.ts: every one of the 10,296 pairs has its one route, each step along a parent edge, its length the depths less twice the meeting's; the longest is 9, entity to burgundy (two deep leaves meet at color or below); the falsifier, burgundy given blue as a second parent, has two routes up to color, one again when taken away. Recorded in space/PORTAL.md. Not adopted, against the data: the hue as the part of speech (all are nouns; the hue is the colour's own, its angle, as map.ts places it, the depth its distance out); routes by hypernym, hyponym, meronym and holonym (one relation, the hypernym; meronym and holonym absent); polytron's "wordnet-to-geometry.c" (polytron has wordnet-shm.c); the tetrahedron's 4 faces as 4 relations and 6 edges as 6 paths (nothing derives it).

narr
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.000
Metaverse Kit's narrative world compiler ported native, its step 4 (mk-port; the author: "lets finsish metaverse kit becuase it has all these tools"): PLAN9/src/znarr.c after tools/mv-narrative-world-compile/index.py, emit (a narrative YAML to narrative-world.v0.json, .asg.json, .timeline.ndjson and .manifest.json) and validate. Literal, the author's choice as for checkpoint, time and shards: the tool stamps the world and the manifest with a sha256 of themselves and names the source by its sha256; here the world names its source by its path, the manifest keeps the source and the counts, and validate recompiles the source and compares the four files byte for byte, the world being the compile of its source. The YAML read as PyYAML 6.0.1 reads the narratives: block mappings and lists, flow lists and mappings, double- and single-quoted and plain scalars, with YAML 1.1's resolution where the compiler writes str() of a scalar (yes, on True; No, OFF False; ~, null None; 007 7 and 017 15, octal; 0x1F 31; 1_000 1000; 0o17 a string; floats as Python's repr); JSON as json.dumps with ensure_ascii off. in/narr written on the host by in/narr/gen.py with the kit's own compiler: the author's full narrative (speaker-for-the-untitled, Narrative Series/NARRATIVE_SCENE.md, The Post-Babel Dialogues: 8 scenes, 8 characters; published here at the author's word), the kit's sample, 60 narratives drawn in the same YAML (scalars, escapes, Unicode, broken chains, missing settings), the compiler's 7 refusals each with its own message, and validate after each of the four files changed or removed: 77 cases, 70 compiled, 280 files. On the host with the libc shim (Rune, getwd, nelem added) and in the guest (src/narr.rc), all 77 agree. The falsifier, znarr -n validate, the files' presence only: all 4 changed outputs pass. Found in the tool, shown on the host: a world retitled "A Different Story", its digest and the manifest's recomputed, passes the tool's validate, a digest proving only that a file matches a digest beside it; the port refuses it, world literal mismatch. On the way, my driver's: rc's backquote split with ifs a space keeps a line's newline on its last word, so the tamper's "drop" and every refusal's message failed to compare in the first guest runs (the falsifier counted 8 for 4); split on the newline too. The limit: anchors, tags, multi-line scalars, dates, sexagesimal numbers, .inf and .nan, keys that are not strings. The spine's 37th phase: 37 of 37 pass, 37 of 37 the same twice. Left of the kit: its other tools (tools/, 46 more), and the Commons reading the world as it reads agentd.

commons-world
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:53.000
The Commons reads the Plan 9 world, the last of Metaverse Kit's step 3 (mk-world: "the Commons reading this world as it reads agentd"; the author: "lets finsish metaverse kit"): boxedPINE c99c67d. The portal (space/src/portal.ts) gains the world: a second Web Lock elects one page to read mkd (?world=, normally 127.0.0.1:18001) every two seconds, its tiles and each tile's state, and every page is told (onWorld); the portal's panel names each tile and its live nodes. space/src/world.ts is now the one reader of a tile's state (zmk's line a node), shared with the Living World (metaverse.ts). The Commons (hub.ts) shows the world at its Living world station: above the orb, each tile a layer, its live nodes at their x and z fitted to the tile, each coloured by its kind as the Living World colours it. Checked in headless Chrome against the running world: index.html read 2 tiles and 4 live nodes, the 3 and 1 that mkd's own states give (demo z0/x0/y0 and z0/x1/y0); metaverse.html still drew z0/x0/y0's 401 events, 3 nodes live and 6 deleted. A browser view, not a port, so not a spine phase: the spine stays at 37. Metaverse Kit's port steps 1 to 4 are done; left, its other tools (tools/), each a phase of its own.

