The grammar of the views, the first step of the views as runes (the author: "lets proceed with your plan", the page files the literal and the CDN scripts provenance, "i dont know if we can port their specif code": carried, not ported; then, of the runes, "to bind to like attributes for creting templates for literal reg expressions", with Wikipedia's note that WSN leaves letter and character undefined, to the language). Measured first, from the 33 pages of unbound/HTML: 67 element names (HTML; inline SVG, its filters camel-cased, feGaussianBlur feMerge feMergeNode, in wesiri.html; A-Frame's a-scene a-entity a-sky), 2,041 attribute values double-quoted, 12 unquoted, 20 bare, none single-quoted; <!DOCTYPE html> 24 times, <!doctype html> 9; 70 comments; few entities (&gt; &amp; &nbsp; &lt; &#39; &times; &copy;); no BOM, no CRLF, non-ASCII in 27 pages; and one page cut off: Tetrahedral Consciousness Universe - Interactive Browser Demo.html ends inside a script line ("node"), its script, body and html never closed (the "(1)" copy beside it whole). Carried from elsewhere, provenance not code: three.js r128 and its OrbitControls, Tailwind, d3 7.9.0, A-Frame 1.5.0, paho-mqtt 1.0.1, libp2p 0.46.4, it-pipe 3.0.1, es-module-shims 1.6.3, and Google Fonts. PLAN9/wsn/html.wsn is the grammar of a page's pieces (COMMENT, DOCTYPE, STARTTAG with its ATTRs and VALUEs, ENDTAG, TEXT, RAWTEXT, ENTITY) over a cast alphabet, letter and character defined for this grammar as WSN leaves them to the language: a letter its lower case, a digit itself, each of < > / = " ' - _ : . # & ; ! itself, whitespace a space, every other rune "~"; the cast is for the question only, a piece being its span of the page's own runes. PLAN9/wsn/html.rules says what a context-free grammar cannot: where a piece ends (a comment at its first "-->", a tag at its ">" outside a quoted value), raw text (script, style, title, textarea) to the first end of its own name, nesting (an end tag closes the nearest open element of its name; void and self-closed elements open none; elements open at the end stay open), and entities read when a view asks. in/views/pieces.mjs reads a page into pieces in one pass; all 33 pages give back their bytes from their 6,944 pieces, and the 112 raw texts hold no end of their own name. In the guest (src/views.rc), zlazy asks each of the 1,739 distinct pieces of its production: wsn/html.wsn accepts all 1,739; the falsifier, each piece asked of the wrong production (a tag, comment or doctype as TEXT, a text run as STARTTAG), accepts none. Pieces are asked one at a time because zlazy keeps for each production and place a table as long as the sentence, so a whole page (6K to 325K runes) is beyond it; the pieces are at most 763 runes, raw text excepted. Found on the way, of the bases: every page's WSN base (wsn/unbound/HTML, FILE = HEAD, a literal a line) drops the page's empty lines, a WSN literal being never empty, and 16 also drop lines of spaces alone, so a base is the page's lines, not the page; the page file stays the literal (the author's word), the bases as they are. And zhtml.c (2026-10-28) already reads the pages through Plan 9's libhtml, a rendering model, the second reading the lazy tree is to be compared with. Three pages name proofs (I-229, I-230), whose records carry their tags in the JS proof index. Next: the tree in omi-types beside OMI.WSN, built lazily by html.rules over these pieces, and the runes bound as attributes for templates that are literal regular expressions. zwsn reads wsn/html.wsn by WSN's own grammar: 25 productions. The spine's 48th phase: 48 of 48 pass, 48 of 48 the same twice.

view-tree
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:05.000
The tree of the views, built lazily (the author: "yes thats perfect"): OMI.View in omi-types (boxedPINE types, omi-types 4ef8f73; boxedPINE records it). A page is its bytes; a Rune the bytes of one UTF-8 sequence, read as chartorune reads them; the page is read into pieces by wsn/html.rules and the pieces into a tree by its nesting rule (an end tag closes the nearest open element of its name and every element opened after it; void and self-closed elements open none; an element open at the end stays open). Nothing is read before it is asked for, nothing twice: Haskell's call-by-need, the two rules of Henderson and Morris that OMI.WSN's table and zlazy keep. In omi-types' own types (Bit, Nibble, Byte; no host Int or String), its constructors hidden. Its tests read the 33 pages of unbound/HTML: each is its 6,944 pieces and its tree flattened, byte for byte, the tree holding the pieces in order; OMI.WSN's accepts takes all 1,739 distinct pieces of their own production of html.wsn, as zlazy did in the guest, and none of the wrong one; the page cut off in a script leaves exactly its script, body and html open and every other page none, as the survey's count said before the tree was written; an endless page opens its first element on its start tag and reads its text only as far as asked, and a page that fails after its first pieces still gives them. One rule stated more exactly on the way, in in/views/pieces.mjs: a quote opens a tag's value when the nearest rune before it, spaces aside, is "=" (it had looked back eight runes); every piece of the 33 pages the same. Next: the same tree in Plan 9 C, its outline compared line for line with OMI.View's, and the second reading, libhtml's (zhtml.c), set beside it.

tree
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:06.000
The tree of the views in Plan 9 C (the author: "yes lets proceed"): PLAN9/src/zviews.c reads a page as OMI.View does, step for step, its runes (the bytes a lead byte calls for, under 0x80 alone, 110xxxxx one more, 1110xxxx two, 11110xxx three, any other alone, taken whether or not they continue, as OMI.View takes them), its pieces by wsn/html.rules 1 and 2, its tree by rule 3, and writes OMI.View's outline: a line a piece, two spaces a level, the production, a space, the piece's bytes (a line feed written \n, a backslash \\), an element left open marked OPEN at its children's level. The goldens are OMI.View's own outlines, by in/views/Outline.hs (built with ghc over omi-types' src), written with in/views/tree.mjs. The falsifier: each page cut by its first end tag (as pieces.mjs reads it), OMI.View's outline of the cut page, which must not be the page's own; the elements left open go from 3 in the pages to 87 in the cut pages, the first end tag being a title's in 27 pages and a style's in 6, whose raw text then runs on to the next end of its name or the page's end. The input tar holds 37.3M of its 64M. On the host (the libc shim, a bio.h over stdio added) and in the guest (src/tree.rc), zviews gives OMI.View's outline of all 33 pages and all 33 cut pages, byte for byte, and no cut page's outline is its page's: the Haskell type and the Plan 9 C read the same tree. Next: the second reading, libhtml's (zhtml.c), set beside it, then the templates over the bound pieces as literal regular expressions. The spine's 49th phase: 49 of 49 pass, 49 of 49 the same twice.

libhtml
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.000
The second reading of the views set beside the tree (the author: "yes continue"): Plan 9's libhtml, a rendering model, reads a page into items (text runs, rules, images, form fields, tables, floats, spacers), not an element tree, so the two meet where both can: the words a reader sees. htmlfs (2026-10-28) serves libhtml's text runs at /n/html; zviews -w gives the tree's words as libhtml renders them, and zviews -s splits libhtml's text the same way (a word a line, at white space and U+00A0, which libhtml writes for &nbsp;). Read first, on the host, from libhtml's text of all 33 pages fetched from the guest: the tree's text pieces, entities read, gave libhtml's words on 27 pages; the other 6 differed in three ways, each libhtml's rendering, found by the differences, not predicted: a bullet "•" text run before each list item (5, 4 and 4, one for each <li>), no words from within a table (libhtml's table item holds its cells: Fano Constants' a⊕b⊕c row, omi-dom-stack-full's vertex and band tables), and a textarea's content as text, where html.rules reads it raw (UBHP's four, the Reality Compiler's two). With the three, all 33 pages give libhtml's words; the falsifier, each left out (-T, -B, -A), was filed on the host before the guest ran (in/views/libhtml): -T differs on exactly 2 pages, -B on 3, -A on 2. In the guest (src/libhtml.rc), as filed: 33 of 33 the same, each falsifier on exactly its pages. The tree is a grammar's reading and libhtml a renderer's, and they agree on every word the pages show, the three rendering choices named. The machine restarted on the way and the libc shim went with the scratchpad again, rebuilt (u.h, libc.h with runetochar and ARGBEGIN, bio.h); and src/render.rc was my first name for the phase, near zrender (the codex renderer of figures.rc), so renamed libhtml.rc before anything was committed. Next: the templates over the bound pieces, as literal regular expressions. The spine's 50th phase: 50 of 50 pass, 50 of 50 the same twice.

match
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:08.000
Templates over the bound pieces, literal regular expressions (the author: "yes", to the three points settled first: the pattern language regexp(2) as it is, nothing stated beside it, regex as it is being the author's word of 2026-10-09; the spans the pieces and each start tag's attributes, the author's "bind to like attributes"; and the falsifier's spans filed exactly, fewer, more or the same, not assumed fewer). The spans: in/views/pieces.mjs reads each start tag's attributes by html.wsn (STARTTAG = "<" NAME { SPACES ATTR } [ SPACES ] [ "/" ] ">", ATTR = ATTRNAME [ [ SPACES ] "=" [ SPACES ] VALUE ]) over the cast, each attribute an ATTR span, its ATTRNAME and its VALUE (its quotes in it), at the page's rune offsets: all 1,920 start tags read, none refused, 2,073 attributes, 2,053 values (2,041 double-quoted and 12 unquoted, as the survey counted, and 20 bare names); no unquoted value runs into a "/>", where html.rules' self-closing and an unquoted value's "/" could have read one rune two ways. A template is a line, its production, a tab, its pattern over the cast; it names each span of its production whose cast the pattern matches, by its rune offsets and its runes. The 317 templates were taken from the pages, not written: each element's start tag (66, ^<name[ />]), each attribute name (88, ^name$), each value found on two pages or more (163, ^value$), "." the one cast symbol regex reads otherwise, written \.; together they name 4,980 spans. in/views/match.mjs found what each names on each page by JavaScript's RegExp; PLAN9/src/zviews.c -m by Plan 9's regexp(2). The falsifier: two mutants of each template, its last symbol the next in the cast alphabet and its first symbol "." (any), 634 in all, their spans filed on the host before the guest ran: 311 name fewer spans, 23 more, 300 the same (a first symbol already alone in its place). On the host (the shim's regexp(2) over POSIX's extended expressions) and in the guest (src/match.rc), regexp(2) names RegExp's spans with their runes, byte for byte, for every template and every mutant on all 33 pages: two regex variants, the same results on these patterns, the agreement the author's word asks. The input tar holds 37.8M of its 64M. The spine's 51st phase: 51 of 51 pass, 51 of 51 the same twice.

spans
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:09.000
The attribute spans in omi-types, the three readings of the views' spans agreeing (a relay the author pasted: "the attribute spans exist in pieces.mjs and zviews.c, but not in OMI.View ... the two readings should agree on the spans as well as on the tree"). OMI.View (omi-types 9730522, boxedPINE f6e8c08) reads a start tag's attributes by html.wsn's ATTR, ATTRNAME and VALUE, in its own types, each span its runes and the runes of its piece before it (a position as the runes before it, not a number); spans gives a page's pieces in order, each start tag followed by its attributes' parts. in/views/match.mjs writes each page's spans, a line each (production, rune offsets), and the same files are checked three ways: by OMI.View in omi-types' tests (test/data/views/spans), by zviews -p in the guest (src/match.rc), and by pieces.mjs, which wrote them. On the 33 pages, 13,143 spans (6,944 pieces, 2,073 attributes and their names, 2,053 values), the same productions and rune offsets in all three; each attribute part's runes are its tag's at its place, the pieces being the page's by the flatten check. And the grammar's own: OMI.WSN's accepts takes all 2,206 distinct attribute spans of their own production of html.wsn; the falsifier, a quoted value asked as an ATTRNAME and an attribute with its "=" asked as a VALUE, refuses all 2,105. On the \. of the templates, asked to be recorded: "." is the one symbol of the cast alphabet that regex reads as other than itself, so a template naming a literal "." writes \. (recorded in the match cue, in/views/match.mjs and zviews.c); the other regex metacharacters cast to "~" and never occur in a cast. The spine: 51 of 51 pass, 51 of 51 the same twice.

polywsn
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:10.000
The poly WSN grammar, its Haskell type and its PIXELATE cast, the second of what the author named after the kit (the author, of the poly tools, 2026-11-01: "yes we handle conversion of polynomials of WSN and Haskell typecast to PIXELATE of light cones"; today, of the dominoes and what followed: "yeah this is featured in the project polytron but lets continue", polytron's dev-docs/TypesOnTypes.md typing polyforms as polynomials, "Rows = Degree (vertical complexity), Columns = Terms (horizontal breadth), Shape = Truth table configuration", polyform_type(domino, 2, [pair])). PLAN9/wsn/poly.wsn is the written form of a polynomial over F2 in x1 to x6 as Metaverse Kit's tools/wave28-poly-lib.js writes and reads it: "0", or terms joined by "+", a term "1" or its variables joined by "*" in increasing order (XONE to XSIX, a term from x1 on and so to x6; my first names M1 to M6 were refused by the guest's WSN reader, an identifier being letters only, and the first run accepted nothing, which the yes side caught). PLAN9/wsn/poly.rules says what it does not: the terms in increasing order of their masks (x_i the bit i - 1, the library sorting the masks' bit strings, their order as numbers), none twice. in/polywsn/gen.mjs, with the library itself read beside it (its refusals exit the process, read with the exit thrown): 175 polynomials the library writes and reads back (in/mvpoly's literals, each of the 64 terms, all 64 at 577 characters, 120 drawn), 4,045 written wrongly and refused by it (a variable out of order or twice, x0 or x7, an empty term, "0" or "1" joined to a term), 286 out of order or with a term twice, refused by it by the rules. In the guest (src/polywsn.rc), zlazy: poly.wsn accepts all 175, refuses all 4,045, and accepts all 286, left to the rules. OMI.Poly in omi-types (2443c96; boxedPINE 357df1b): a Term the 6-bit mask, a Poly the set of its terms, only ever canonical; readPoly says written, not written, out of order or repeated, as the library refuses, on all 4,506, and writePoly gives the 175 back byte for byte; OMI.WSN's accepts answers poly.wsn as zlazy did. The cast, my reading of the author's "PIXELATE of light cones", to be confirmed: the 64 points of x1 to x6 as pixels, each term lighting the points at or above it (its light cone, upward in the 6-cube; 2^(6 - k) of them for a term of k variables, and only those), a polynomial the XOR of its terms' cones; unpixelate reads the terms back the same way downward, every one of the 175 from its pixels; a sum's cast the XOR of the casts; "1" lights all 64, and all 64 terms together light the origin alone. The spine's 52nd phase: 52 of 52 pass, 52 of 52 the same twice.

pixelate
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:11.000
PIXELATE, the poly cast in Plan 9 C (the author, of the pixels and the spectrum, pixelate-fft: "Its both together its like the pi being resolved from the inceide in the omi pi proof, its casual"). Read first in the proof: proofs/coq/03-projection/PiProjectionPreservesWitnesses.v builds its Leibniz terms from the incidence phase schedule alone, each sign the parity of its phase, each denominator 2n + 1 from the BQF bridge, the partial sums bracketing pi, which enters only at the limit. So the cast keeps the inside literal: the pixels, causal (each term lighting its light cone, a pixel only from the terms below it, by the 6-stage butterfly), and the spectrum, the binary FFT (Walsh-Hadamard), whose signs are parity phases with no pi in them; in omi-types' OMI.Poly (79c2d02; boxedPINE 47fb1a4) a bin is kept as bits, the points where the pixels and the linear function a.x differ, and its number, 64 - 2 x their count, is left to a projection. PLAN9/src/zpixelate.c reads each line by poly.wsn and poly.rules and gives its 64 pixels and 64 bins, or why it is refused (not written, out of order, repeated). in/pixelate/gen.mjs, with the kit's library reading each line: the 4,506 lines of in/polywsn, 175 written, 4,045 not written, 111 out of order, 175 repeated. On the host (the shim) and in the guest (src/pixelate.rc), zpixelate gives every line as filed, byte for byte; OMI.Poly gives the same 4,506 lines in omi-types' tests; the falsifier, zpixelate -n, the cones taken downward (the past cone), gives the lines filed for it, differing on all 175 written. The spine's 53rd phase: 53 of 53 pass, 53 of 53 the same twice.
